# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
# Expected frame hashes for probe/c/vdisp-frame.c, one line per resolution:
#
#     <width>x<height> <FNV-1a-64 of the pattern, hex>
#
# The hash is a pure function of the size -- vdisp-frame's pattern() is
# f(x, y) and nothing else -- so this file can be regenerated on any machine,
# with no GPU and no virtual display:
#
#     cc -O2 -o vdisp-frame probe/c/vdisp-frame.c
#     ./vdisp-frame --reference 1600x900
#
# WHY IT IS COMMITTED. Inside a run the probe already compares what it wrote
# against what it read back, and that catches a corrupted round trip. What it
# cannot catch is the pattern itself changing: edit pattern() and both halves
# move together, exactly the way the display gate's EDID comparison moves
# when nvrm_edid.c changes. This file is the third party. A gate run against
# a stale line here fails and names the command above, which is the intended
# outcome -- somebody has to look at why the pixels are different.
#
# 1600x900 is the vdisplay gate's default (scripts/test.sh vdisplay), chosen because it is NOT
# the module's own 1920x1080 default: a size that comes out right proves the
# vdisplay_width/height parameters reached the EDID rather than that a
# default survived. 1920x1080 is here so the default configuration can be
# gated too.
1600x900 0xb6a79817d7f4a5c3
1920x1080 0x4666b6856937e583
